# Summary
Introduced. The commit reorganizes the settings page by replacing the old auto-post tab and its folder/interval controls with only high-level toggles and webhook inputs, while leaving hidden configuration fields active and saved. This creates a config-import review bypass for security-sensitive settings.
This commit removes the auto-post settings tab that previously exposed `autoPhoto.photoDirectory`, `screenshotAutoPost.screenshotDirectory`, and scan intervals, and it also removes the maximum input size control. However, the frontend still submits the entire `state.config` object to the backend. Those hidden JSON fields are still parsed, normalized only lightly, persisted, and used by `restartAutoPhotoWatcher` to start background watchers. In the threat model, third-party config JSON is attacker-controlled if the user opens it. After this change, a malicious config can set `output.uploadDiscord=true`, set a Discord webhook, enable auto processing, and set a hidden watch directory such as a broad Pictures/Desktop folder. The UI only shows generic auto-processing toggles and password-style webhook fields, so the user has no meaningful way to inspect or correct the watched directory before saving. Once saved, future supported images created in that hidden directory are automatically processed and uploaded to the configured webhook.

